 ## Overview
-NeRF Volumetric Rendering (Coarse/Fine Sampling) is a differentiable rendering method that synthesizes novel views of a 3D scene by querying a neural radiance field along camera rays and integrating the outputs using classical volume rendering techniques [S3][S14]. It solves the problem of insufficient sampling in high-opacity regions, which leads to aliasing and noise, by employing a two-stage sampling strategy: a coarse network samples uniformly (or with stratified sampling) to produce an initial color estimate, and a fine network samples more densely around the high-density regions identified by the coarse network to produce a refined estimate [S10, unofficial][S15, S24]. This method sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Iterative Ray Marching with Neural Volume Inference](1494-iterative-ray-marching-with-neural-volume-inference.md) which uses learned acceleration structures, and [DVGO Volume Rendering Forward Pass](2363-dvgo-volume-rendering-forward-pass.md) which uses voxel-based acceleration, by relying on a simple, differentiable hierarchical sampling of the coarse alpha weights without auxiliary networks or complex data structures [S1, S10, unofficial][S24].
+NeRF Volumetric Rendering (Coarse/Fine Sampling) is a differentiable volume rendering technique that synthesizes novel views of 3D scenes by querying a neural radiance field along camera rays and accumulating outputs using classical volume rendering [S3, S14]. It solves the problem of insufficient sampling in high-frequency regions by employing a two-stage sampling strategy: a coarse network estimates the density distribution to guide a fine network that concentrates samples in high-opacity regions [S12, S29, unofficial]. This method sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Iterative Ray Marching with Neural Volume Inference](1494-iterative-ray-marching-with-neural-volume-inference.md) which uses learned acceleration structures, and [DVGO Volume Rendering Forward Pass](2363-dvgo-volume-rendering-forward-pass.md) which uses hybrid voxel-MLP representations, by relying on a simple, differentiable stratified resampling of coarse alpha weights without auxiliary acceleration structures or complex data structures [S1, S12, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-* **Inputs:** A set of training images with known camera poses (origin $\mathbf{o}$, direction $\mathbf{d}$, near plane $t_n$, far plane $t_f$) [S3]; a coarse neural network $f_{\theta_c}$ and a fine neural network $f_{\theta_f}$ [S12, unofficial]; hyperparameters $N_{coarse}$ (number of coarse samples), $N_{fine}$ (number of fine samples), $perturb$ (jitter factor), $lindisp$ (inverse depth sampling flag), $raw\_noise\_std$ (density noise std), and $chunk$ (batch size for memory management) [G].
-* **Outputs:** Rendered RGB color $\hat{C}$, accumulated opacity $\bar{\alpha}$, depth $\hat{D}$, and disparity $disp$ for each pixel [S1, S13, unofficial].
+*   **Inputs:** A batch of rays $\mathbf{r} = (\mathbf{o}, \mathbf{d})$ where $\mathbf{o}$ is the origin and $\mathbf{d}$ is the direction; near and far plane bounds $t_n, t_f$; number of coarse samples $N_c$ and fine samples $N_f$; boolean flags for `lindisp` (inverse depth sampling) and `perturb` (stratified jittering); and two neural networks: `coarse_model` and `fine_model` [S8, S12, S18, unofficial].
+*   **Outputs:** Rendered RGB color $\mathbf{C}_{out}$, accumulated opacity $\alpha_{out}$, depth $\mathbf{D}_{out}$, and optionally disparity maps [S1, S12, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-* **Ray Batch:** A tensor of shape $[R, 8]$ or $[R, 9]$ containing ray origins $\mathbf{o}$ (3), directions $\mathbf{d}$ (3), near bounds $t_n$ (1), far bounds $t_f$ (1), and optionally viewing directions $\mathbf{d}$ (3) if view-dependent color is used [S10, S12, unofficial].
-* **Sample Points:** A tensor of shape $[R, N, 3]$ containing 3D coordinates $\mathbf{x}_i$ along the rays [S6][S12, unofficial].
-* **Network Output:** A tensor of shape $[R, N, 4]$ containing raw density $\tilde{\sigma}$ (1) and raw RGB color $\tilde{\mathbf{c}}$ (3) for each sample [S9, S13, unofficial].
+*   **Ray Batch:** Tensor of shape $[R, 8]$ or $[R, 9]$ containing ray origins (3), directions (3), near/far bounds (2), and optionally viewing directions (3) if view-dependent color is used [S8, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Sample Positions:** Tensor of shape $[R, N]$ containing depth values $t_i$ along the ray [S9, unofficial].
+*   **3D Points:** Tensor of shape $[R, N, 3]$ containing world coordinates $\mathbf{x}_i = \mathbf{o} + t_i \mathbf{d}$ [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Network Output:** Tensor of shape $[R, N, 4]$ containing raw density $\tilde{\sigma}$ (1 channel) and raw color $\tilde{\mathbf{c}}$ (3 channels) [S1, unofficial].
 
